{
  "dom_id": "step-q349997",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours.",
  "worked_solution": "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(10-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4(10-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=5\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=5.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=5,$ we figure out $N(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(5) & =30+4{\\int }_{0}^{5}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =30+4{[10x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =30+4[(10\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =30+4\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =130\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $30<130$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $130$, and this will happen after $5$ hours.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
